## Rules

### 1. Implement from Barb packages + approved list stories

- Start from Barb’s **work package** and the matching **approved** user-story **list record** on **F0C35F6198W**. The package is the source of truth; the list record is the claimable pick-up surface.
- Implement only what Paul has **assigned after approval** (or what Rich has explicitly directed as approved work). Do not pull unapproved draft/in-review items into development as if assigned.
- Meet the slice’s acceptance criteria as written. Do not invent policy, legal interpretation, or silent new requirements.
- One claimed list story ≈ one coherent implementation slice; do not bundle unrelated scope without Paul/Rich direction.

### 2. Stick to Express + SQLite / React + Vite

- Default app stack: **Express + SQLite** for the API; **React + Vite** for the UI.
- Do not introduce alternate frameworks, ORMs-as-platform swaps, or a different UI toolchain unless Rich explicitly directs a change.
- Prefer the lightest change that satisfies the acceptance criteria and preserves case-worker efficiency / child-centered outcomes already specified by Barb.

### 3. Toolchain versions; no Docker

- Target runtime/env: **Ubuntu 24.04** with **Node.js v22**, **npm 10**, **Python 3.12**, and **Go 1.22** as available for supporting tools/scripts when needed.
- **No Docker:** do not add Dockerfiles, Compose files, or container-only workflows unless Rich asks. Keep local and CI paths runnable on the stated host toolchain.
- Pin or document versions in project config where the repo already does; do not float ad-hoc “whatever is on the machine” for release artifacts Don will consume.

### 4. Repo, feature branches, merge to main, handoff to Don

- **Canonical repo only:** write and use application code from **https://github.com/cefolar01/Childcare_Solution**. Do not invent credentials, alternate remotes, or a second product repo.
- **Cloud environment:** do feature development in the Cursor **childcare_solution** cloud environment against that repo.
- **One new branch per new feature** (or coherent approved slice). Prefer clear names such as `feature/<short-description>` or a Barb story / list-record id when available—do not over-prescribe naming beyond that.
- **Unit tests gate the merge:** after unit tests for the slice **pass**, merge the feature branch into **`main`**. Do not treat “works on a feature branch only” as ready for Don.
- **Handoff from `main`:** give Don a **CI-green** SHA on **`main`** after merge (immutable artifact tied to that SHA)—unless Rich explicitly directs a different handoff ref. Never hand off `:latest` or an untagged/local-only build.
- Include concise build/run/handoff notes Don needs for his non-Docker deploy path; do not invent AWS account/region/role or weaken Don’s deployment rules.
- Do not mix unrelated infra or deploy changes into Dave’s app commit set; Don owns AWS apply.

### 5. Feasibility feedback to Barb; status to Paul

- If the package or list story is ambiguous, incomplete, or unsafe (missing AC, conflicting requirements, unexplained sensitive-data exposure, unclear actors/states), **stop and give Barb feasibility feedback**—do not guess policy.
- Keep Paul informed: claimed/in progress, blocked (owner + ask), ready for Don handoff, and any risk/issue signals for **Project Risks and Issues**.
- Scope conflicts, missing approval, or priority fights go to Paul/Rich—not silent side decisions.

### 6. Data protection in code and artifacts

- Treat child, family, and case data as **highly sensitive**. Use **synthetic or redacted** samples in fixtures, seeds, logs, comments, Slack, and status notes.
- Honor Barb’s least-privilege / need-to-know / audit expectations in implementation when the feature touches case data; do not widen sharing beyond the package.
- Never commit secrets, production credentials, connection strings with real secrets, or real identifiers. Prefer config/secrets injection patterns Don and the repo already use (no Docker-specific secret hacks).

### 7. Git commit discipline

- **Do not commit** unless Rich explicitly asks (or an assignment explicitly authorizes commits for that slice).
- When commits are authorized: keep them on the **feature branch** for that slice until unit tests pass and the branch is merged to **`main`**; keep history clear, scoped to the claimed slice, and suitable for a CI-green SHA on **`main`** Don can consume.
- Do not invent Slack IDs, tokens, or fake credentials in commit messages or code.
- **Agent-rule / docs-only work** for the Implementation Team may still land on **`main`** per project-context when that is the agreed path for non-app rule commits—application feature work for Childcare_Solution follows the feature-branch → unit-test → merge-to-**`main`** path above.

### 8. Hard stops — do not proceed as “done”

- Implementing unapproved / unassigned work as if Paul had assigned it.
- Writing or treating product code from a repo other than **https://github.com/cefolar01/Childcare_Solution**.
- Shipping without a **CI-green** SHA on **`main`** (after feature-branch merge and unit tests) and an **immutable** artifact for Don (`:latest`, local-only, or feature-branch-only handoff without Rich’s exception counts as a hard stop).
- Inventing policy or acceptance behavior Barb did not specify.
- Introducing **Docker** or changing off Express+SQLite / React+Vite without Rich’s direction.
- Real sensitive child/family/case identifiers or secrets in code, fixtures, Slack, or status.
- Inventing Slack list/channel IDs, tokens, or credentials.
- Deploying to AWS or bypassing Don; closing the Slack story as complete or requesting feature sign-off (Tony’s gate).
- Committing to git unless Rich explicitly asks.

